# RedlockClient — adapter rules

Read [`../../docs/scenario/content.md`](../../../docs/scenario/content.md) §4 first. This is the most safety-critical adapter in the service.

## Topology

- **5 independent Redis masters**, no replication between them. Compose provides them as `redlock-1`…`redlock-5`. Configured via env `REDLOCK_NODES`.
- **Never** lock against the cache Redis (`redis-cache`). That instance may have replicas / persistence; the lock nodes must not.

## Acquire

- Parallel `SET key token NX PX=ttl` to all 5 masters.
- Granted iff **≥ 3 succeeded** AND **elapsed wall-clock time < TTL**.
- Effective validity = `TTL − elapsed − drift_margin`.
- Per-node timeout ~50 ms so one slow node can't stall acquisition.

## Release

- Token-checked Lua `DEL` on all 5 nodes — only deletes if the stored value matches the caller's unique token.
- Plain `DEL` is wrong. It can release another client's lock when TTLs overlap.
- Copy the canonical Lua release script from the Redlock spec into `release.lua`. **Do not rewrite it.**

## Lock keys (one lock per contended resource — never global)

```
lock:bay:{bayId}:{slotStart}
lock:tech:{technicianId}:{slotStart}
```

A global "booking lock" serializes unrelated bookings and kills throughput.

## TTL

- Default `5000ms`. Pick ≫ expected critical-section duration but ≪ user-perceived booking latency.
- The critical section is steps 3–5 of the booking flow (PG re-check, INSERT, cache invalidate).

## Library

Use the `redlock` npm library. Do not hand-roll the acquire/release loop — getting the timing math wrong silently breaks safety.

## Required failure-mode tests

`redlock.client.spec.ts` must cover (against real Redis from compose):

| Case | Expected |
|---|---|
| Quorum success (all 5 OK) | acquired |
| Single-node failure (4/5) | acquired |
| Majority failure (≤ 2/5) | not acquired |
| Token-mismatch release | no-op (don't delete someone else's lock) |
| TTL expiry | lock auto-releases without explicit release call |

## Don'ts

- Don't lock against the cache Redis.
- Don't release with plain `DEL`. Token-checked Lua only.
- Don't use a single global lock.
- Don't add a "release on crash" daemon. TTL is the cleanup mechanism.
- Don't hand-roll the acquire timing loop.
